(*
ProfileCostNodeC6 — what does an OPEN allocation-attribution window cost, on a
C6, under the real bridge workload? The measurement rete#203 (design
2026-08-12 §9.7) still owes, and the one that must replace D4's refuted ~0.02%
projection.

WHY NOT MethodProfileNodeC6. That node drives Hanoi, which allocates nothing, so
it would time a hook that never fires. The allocation hook only has a cost where
there are allocations, and on this workload there is one arena allocation per 5.6
activations (design §9.2) — three orders of magnitude denser than the host
window, which is exactly why the projection could not be carried forward.

WHY ONE BINARY, ONE BOOT. Non-negotiable, and not a preference: a cross-binary
OFF/ON of the ACTIVATION hook once put the ON build 4% FASTER
(MethodProfileNodeC6.nsl:10) — code-layout roulette, not a result. So both arms
run here, back to back, in one image and one cache state, and the only thing that
differs between them is `g_mprof_enabled`.

THE THREE BLOCKS, AND WHY THE THIRD ONE EXISTS.

  settle -> [ A1 (off) -> B (ON, dump at close) -> A2 (off) ] repeated forever

A1-vs-B alone cannot tell the hook's cost from ordinary drift — mesh conditions,
a retransmit, a GC that happened to land in one block. A2 is the CONTROL: it
costs nothing to run (an OFF arm needs no prim call at all) and it is the only
thing that makes the result falsifiable. If |A2 - A1| is the same size as
|B - A1|, this run measured drift and says nothing about the hook, and the
comparison must be read as refuted rather than as a small effect.

The triple REPEATS, and that is what makes A2 worth having. The first run's single
triple put the drift at a third of the effect — enough to believe the sign, not
enough to publish a percentage. Repeating turns it into a sign test: B is either
the peak of its own triple, round after round, or it is not.

Arms could not simply be ALTERNATED at a finer grain, which would cancel drift
better still: there is no "pause" — `profileStop` disables AND dumps, so every
ON->OFF edge emits ~300 lines down the UART. Once per round the next block's
lead-in absorbs that; once per tick it would be the dominant cost in the
measurement. Anything finer needs a new prim, and the standing preference is
fewer mechanisms.

WHAT IS AND IS NOT BEING MEASURED. The delta here is the cost of a window being
OPEN: the hash-probe-and-accumulate in `mprof_bump`/`mprof_alloc` that only runs
when enabled. It is NOT the cost of the profiler being BUILT IN — the OFF arms
still pay the load-and-branch and the non-inlinable call from gc.c, because those
are compiled in. That second number is a cross-binary question, and cross-binary
is the comparison this file exists not to make.

TWO PROBES, ONE PAIR OF ARMS. The bridge spans price BOTH hooks together — one
flag gates the activation counter and the allocation columns, so no runtime A/B
can separate them. A fixed-depth Hanoi burst rides the same arms in its own span
(`hanoiBurst` below): it activates and allocates nothing, so its delta is the
ACTIVATION hook alone, at a table the bridge has already populated to 0.77 — and
the allocation hook follows by subtraction. That is rete#166's question, and it
is answerable here only because the two workloads share a tick.

NO `dbg/req` REACTION, DELIBERATELY — this is the one difference from
PortaBridgeNodeC6 that is load-bearing rather than cosmetic. The gateway's store
holds a replayable `dbg/req profile` row for this node id, and a cold boot serves
it from cursor 0. Wiring the reaction would open a THIRD profile window, on
someone else's schedule, underneath the arms below — and would re-arm rete#206,
which wedges the node when that window ends. Nothing here writes to porta either,
so no new replay row is created: the spans are read from the table and PRINTED,
not published through SpanCapture.

Otherwise the bridging body is PortaBridgeNodeC6's, unchanged, because the
workload being measured has to be the workload. Dropped from that file: the
TAP-lite self-check (this is a measurement, not a gate) and SpanCapture (see
above). The arm sequencer keys off `bridge acked > 0`, which is the same signal
the self-check's first assertion rests on.

Build and flash (c6spare — never the bell; the interlock in run.sh will refuse
the bell's JTAG, but pin the port anyway so the refusal is never the thing that
decides). run.sh does NOT source the west env, and with two C6s and two CP2102Ns
on the bench it refuses to guess either port — so all four lines are load-bearing:
  source ~/zephyr-esp32/.venv/bin/activate
  source ~/zephyr-esp32/zephyr/zephyr-env.sh
  export ESPTOOL_PORT=/dev/serial/by-id/usb-Espressif_USB_JTAG_serial_debug_unit_40:4C:CA:44:6E:60-if00
  export CONSOLE_PORT=/dev/serial/by-id/usb-Silicon_Labs_CP2102N_USB_to_UART_Bridge_Controller_d2e21074c7beed11a5935a4371c9e8b5-if00-port0
  OEVM_METHOD_PROFILE=1 bash run.sh --board esp32c6 --node ProfileCostNodeC6.nsl --flash \
    --conf-extra "prj_esp32c6_thread.conf;prj_esp32c6_thread_ftd.conf"

THE `--conf-extra` IS NOT OPTIONAL. `prj_esp32c6.conf` carries no OpenThread; the
radio arrives only through that flag, and without it this node builds, boots,
prints READY and never attaches — so every arm below measures a bridge talking to
nobody. It cost the bench a night once already (plan 2026-08-06 step 2).

`OEVM_METHOD_PROFILE=1` is the rest of it: `profileStart`/`profileStop` are
no-ops without it and the arms would be indistinguishable — silently, since a
no-op prim answers normally. No RUNTIME_GATE and no FLIP_AT: those flip on an
activation COUNT, and this file flips on a block boundary it can name in the log.

expect (console):
PROFILE COST NODE READY
tick 1 misses 0 acked 0 dropped 0
...
round 1 arm 1 off-a begin
round 1 arm 1 off-a block ms 60000 ticks 30 misses 0 dropped 0
round 1 arm 1 off-a span jobTick n 30 sumLo ... sumHi 0 min ... max ...
...
round 1 arm 2 on begin
...
mprof[window]: methods=... activations=... overflow=0 cap=256
mprofa[window]: ...
round 1 arm 3 off-b begin
...
round 2 arm 1 off-a begin

Dialect: `[ ... ] whileTrue:`, never `repeat`; state in instance slots so every
reaction block is a one-line self-send.
*)
class ProfileCostNodeC6 usingPlatform: platform net: net = (
  | space bridge timers ticks ::= 0.
    spans sTick sCheckIn sPublish sHanoi
    (* the arm sequencer. phase 0 = settling, 1 = off-a, 2 = on, 3 = off-b,
       then round + 1 and back to 1. `round` is on every printed line, because
       a triple is only a triple if its three blocks can be paired back up. *)
    phase ::= 0. round ::= 0. blockTick ::= 0. settleCount ::= 0. blockT0 ::= 0. |
) (
  (* rete#186: THE ID IS THE BOARD, and it is baked at build time rather than written
     here. This method used to answer a string literal, and the literal was the bug:
     whatever devkit was flashed with this app wrote into that one space, so `c6-bench`
     accumulated 36,153 events across 19 `obs/build` entries from at least two boards
     AND two different apps, with nothing in the store saying which. `platform nodeId`
     answers the board's factory link address (rete#118: read from silicon, 12 hex
     characters; the build-time define is gone), so two boards flashed from one image
     get two porta spaces. The bench name travels separately, as the optional `board`
     field of `obs/build`, from run.sh's OEVM_BOARD_NAME.
     The ROLE is not gone, it just lives where it always belonged: porta writes
     `goal/name` and this node converges `obs/name` onto it. *)
  nodeId    = ( ^ platform nodeId )
  gwAddr    = ( ^ 'fdc5:9109:9afe:97ea:9541:d963:f8d9:8567' )
  gwPort    = ( ^ 6969 )
  localPort = ( ^ 16970 )
  cadence   = ( ^ 2000 )   (* ms between check-ins — PortaBridgeNodeC6's, unchanged *)
  chip      = ( ^ #esp32c6 )

  (* Ticks of healthy bridging before the first arm opens. The mesh attach is
     already behind us by then — `bridge acked > 0` is what starts this count —
     so this is settling of the WORKLOAD, not of the radio: the first check-ins
     after an attach carry the whole boot's backlog of cells and are not
     representative of anything that follows. *)
  settleTicks = ( ^ 10 )

  (* Discarded at the head of EVERY block, including the first. Its real job is
     at the head of block 3, where the ~300-line dump has just gone down the UART
     and may have cost a check-in — but it is applied uniformly, because a
     correction applied to one arm and not the others is a bias, not a fix. *)
  leadTicks = ( ^ 3 )

  (* 30 ticks at the 2 s cadence = 60 s of samples per arm, ~200 s for all three.
     Chosen so `publish` — the local, allocation-heavy span, the one whose delta
     should be visible — clears 30 samples rather than for any percentile: 200 is
     Spans>>minSamplesForP95 and nothing here reports a percentile. *)
  blockTicks = ( ^ 30 )

  public main: platform2 args: args = (
    | sock tickChan |
    space:: platform space.
    sock:: net udpBindPort: self localPort.
    sock == nil ifTrue: [ ^ self fail: 'bind' ].
    (* ONE Spans for this image, minted BEFORE the bridge, which registers its
       own six slots at construction. Two instances would both allocate from 2
       and silently share samples. *)
    spans:: platform Spans new.
    bridge:: platform PortaBridge
      socket: sock space: space id: self nodeId peer: self gwAddr port: self gwPort
      spans: spans.
    bridge subscribeStream: 'tel/log'.
    bridge subscribeStream: 'sys/reset'.

    (* rete#273 D2: telCad and the fleet-default miss allowance. *)
    bridge liveness: self cadence misses: 8.

    sTick::     spans register: #jobTick.
    sCheckIn::  spans register: #checkIn.
    sPublish::  spans register: #publish.
    (* rete#166's arm. Slot 14 of OEVM_SPAN_SLOTS = 16 — ONE spare left, and a
       15th registration would silently answer an index the C side ignores. *)
    sHanoi::    spans register: #hanoi.

    (* rete#259: the bridge owns obs/build; #man rides iff the image carries a
       manifest pin (rete#243). *)
    bridge publishBuild: self chip.

    timers:: platform timers.     (* hold ONE instance — it owns the pending registry *)
    space whenever: 'sys/timer' do: [ :e | timers dispatch: e ].
    tickChan:: space channel: 'sys/tick'.
    tickChan whenever: '' do: [ :m | self tick ].
    (* rete#231 D1: the KERNEL owns the period now. The app still owns what a tick
       DOES — its reaction on this channel is unchanged — but the registration is
       the bridge's, so the deadline #215's observer reads from
       timer_target_snapshot exists even for an app that declares no timer of its
       own, and a heartbeat this node stops emitting when it wedges is a heartbeat
       whose absence means something. The bridge is HANDED this instance: two
       Timers alias each other's tokens and drop every dispatch silently. *)
    bridge startTicking: timers on: tickChan.

    'PROFILE COST NODE READY' println.

    [ true ] whileTrue: [
      platform pumpEvents.        (* blocks until the cadence timer fires *)
      space drain ].
    ^ 0
  )

  (* PortaBridgeNodeC6's tick, with the verdict replaced by the arm sequencer.
     `armStep` runs AFTER `spans end: sTick`, so the tick that closes a block is
     itself counted in that block — otherwise the last sample would be the one
     missing. *)
  tick = (
    ticks:: ticks + 1.
    spans begin: sTick.
    spans begin: sCheckIn.
    bridge checkIn.
    spans end: sCheckIn.
    spans begin: sPublish.
    self publishHealth.
    space out: 'tel/log' value:
      (platform Record tag: #Log with: #lvl -> #info
                                 with: #txt -> ('tick ' , ticks printString)).
    spans end: sPublish.
    self report.
    spans end: sTick.
    (* OUTSIDE jobTick, deliberately: this burst is not the workload, it is a
       second probe carried by the same arms, and folding it into the bridge's
       spans would corrupt the very numbers §1 of the result reports. Before
       `armStep`, so a block's last tick contributes its burst like every
       other. *)
    spans begin: sHanoi.
    self hanoiBurst.
    spans end: sHanoi.
    self armStep.
    ^ self
  )

  (* --- rete#166's arm: activations WITHOUT allocations ----------------------
     The bridge spans price both hooks together, because one flag gates both and
     no runtime A/B can split them. This burst splits them by ARITHMETIC instead:
     it activates and (see below) allocates nothing, so its own OFF/ON delta is
     the ACTIVATION hook's price alone — and the allocation hook's follows by
     subtraction from the bridge spans.

     WHY IT RUNS INSIDE THE BRIDGE AND NOT INSTEAD OF IT. #166's complaint about
     the quoted 0.90% is that it was taken at a load factor of 0.008. Hanoi ALONE
     would reproduce exactly that mistake. Run here, `hanoiMove:` is interned into
     a table the bridge has already populated to 0.77 — which is the condition
     #166 asks about, and it is only available because the two workloads share a
     tick.

     THE DENOMINATOR IS ARITHMETIC, NOT AN ESTIMATE. `hanoiMove:` makes exactly
     2^n - 1 activations, so a block of 30 ticks at depth 11 is 30 * 2048
     activations (the burst's own frame included) with nothing to read out of a
     dump and nothing to assume. That is the whole reason this workload and not
     another: it predicts its own activation count.

     DEPTH 11 = 2047 activations ~ 37 ms, under 2% of the 2 s tick — small enough
     that it cannot start costing check-ins, which would put radio back into the
     one span that has none. Deeper buys nothing: the delta is a RATIO and is
     scale-invariant, and the per-bracket overhead (~13 µs, tuvm#61) is already
     four orders below the span.

     "ALLOCATES NOTHING" IS CHECKED, NOT ASSUMED. If `hanoiMove:` allocates — a
     reified block per `ifTrue:` would do it — it appears as an `mprofa:` row like
     anything else, with its own byte and allocation counts, and the subtraction
     uses them. The claim is falsifiable from the same dump that carries the
     measurement, which is why it is safe to state. *)
  hanoiDepth = ( ^ 11 )

  hanoiBurst = ( ^ self hanoiMove: self hanoiDepth from: 1 to: 2 temp: 3 )

  (* Body verbatim from MethodProfileNodeC6.nsl, which took it from
     00_perf/performance.nsl. Kept identical on purpose: the two nodes' numbers
     are comparable only while the workload is the same one. *)
  hanoiMove: n from: src to: dst temp: tmp = (
    n > 1 ifTrue: [
      self hanoiMove: n - 1 from: src to: tmp temp: dst.
      self hanoiMove: n - 1 from: tmp to: dst temp: src.
    ].
    ^ n
  )

  report = (
    ('tick ' , ticks printString ,
     ' misses ' , bridge misses printString ,
     ' acked ' , bridge acked printString ,
     ' dropped ' , bridge dropped printString) println.
    ^ self
  )

  publishHealth = (
    | pairs |
    pairs:: platform OrderedCollection new.
    pairs add: #up -> platform monotonicMillis.
    (* The same latched value the push envelope carries (rete#211); see
       PortaBridgeNode.nsl for why the literal was wrong. *)
    pairs add: #bid -> bridge bootId.
    pairs add: #wak -> ticks.
    pairs add: #pto -> bridge misses.
    pairs add: #sdr -> bridge dropped.
    space put: 'obs/health' value:
      (platform Record tag: #Health fields: (platform Map fromPairs: pairs)).
    ^ self
  )

  fail: msg = ( msg println. ^ 1 )

  (* --- the arm sequencer ---------------------------------------------------
     Each of these is a self-send from `tick`, never inline work in a block. *)

  armStep = (
    phase = 0 ifTrue: [ ^ self settleStep ].
    blockTick:: blockTick + 1.
    (* The lead-in is thrown away by RE-zeroing the table, not by counting from a
       later tick: the samples taken during it are the disturbed ones. *)
    blockTick = self leadTicks ifTrue: [ ^ self openWindow ].
    blockTick >= (self leadTicks + self blockTicks) ifTrue: [ ^ self endBlock ].
    ^ self )

  settleStep = (
    bridge acked > 0 ifTrue: [ settleCount:: settleCount + 1 ].
    settleCount >= self settleTicks ifTrue: [ ^ self beginBlock: 1 ].
    ^ self )

  (* profileStart ZEROES the mprof table and enables it, so it is the arm's own
     start of window and must not be moved out of block 2. *)
  beginBlock: n = (
    phase:: n.
    blockTick:: 0.
    n = 1 ifTrue: [ round:: round + 1 ].
    n = 2 ifTrue: [ platform profileStart ].
    ('round ' , round printString ,
     ' arm ' , n printString , ' ' , (self armName: n) , ' begin') println.
    spans resetAll.
    ^ self )

  (* The lead-in is over: this is where the block's measured window actually
     starts, for the spans. mprof's window started at beginBlock: and so covers
     the lead-in too — deliberately, because zeroing it here would mean calling
     profileStart twice and the second call is indistinguishable from the first
     in the dump. The lead-in's allocations are three ticks out of thirty-three
     and are not being compared to anything. *)
  openWindow = (
    blockT0:: 0 millis.
    spans resetAll.
    ^ self )

  (* Rounds repeat FOREVER. One triple cannot separate the hook from drift: the
     first run put |A2 - A1| at a third of |B - offMean|, which is a ratio, not a
     result. Repeating gives a distribution of triples, and the claim that has to
     survive is "B is the peak of its own triple" round after round — a sign
     test that no single-round arithmetic can fake. Nothing accumulates across
     rounds on the node; the pairing is done by the reader, off the log. *)
  endBlock = (
    | ms |
    ms:: 0 millis - blockT0.
    ('round ' , round printString ,
     ' arm ' , phase printString , ' ' , (self armName: phase) ,
     ' block ms ' , ms printString ,
     ' ticks ' , self blockTicks printString ,
     ' misses ' , bridge misses printString ,
     ' dropped ' , bridge dropped printString) println.
    self printSpans.
    (* AFTER the rows are printed: the dump is ~300 lines and the next block's
       lead-in is what absorbs it. *)
    phase = 2 ifTrue: [ platform profileStop ].
    phase < 3 ifTrue: [ ^ self beginBlock: phase + 1 ].
    ^ self beginBlock: 1 )

  armName: n = (
    n = 1 ifTrue: [ ^ 'off-a' ].
    n = 2 ifTrue: [ ^ 'on' ].
    n = 3 ifTrue: [ ^ 'off-b' ].
    ^ '?' )

  (* Every slot, including one that took no samples — a slot silently skipped
     reads as a slot that was not there, and the bridge's six inner spans are
     exactly where an unexpected zero would matter. Fields are span.h's
     SPAN_STAT_* order, 1-based through basicAt:; `sum` is split at 1e9 by the
     prim (span.c:85) and is reassembled by the reader, not here. *)
  printSpans = (
    | i n s |
    i:: 0. n:: spans slotCount.
    [ i < n ] whileTrue: [
      s:: spans statsAt: i.
      ('round ' , round printString ,
       ' arm ' , phase printString , ' ' , (self armName: phase) ,
       ' span ' , (spans pathAt: i) ,
       ' n ' , (s basicAt: 1) printString ,
       ' sumLo ' , (s basicAt: 4) printString ,
       ' sumHi ' , (s basicAt: 5) printString ,
       ' min ' , (s basicAt: 2) printString ,
       ' max ' , (s basicAt: 3) printString) println.
      i:: i + 1 ].
    ^ self )
) : (
  public packageUsing: manifest = ( ^ self new )
)
